<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Warnings are errors across every project here, and the reason is numeric:
         this port's failure mode is a silent conversion, not a crash. CS0675
         (sign-extended operand in a bitwise or) alone would corrupt bit-packed
         layouts like DisjointSets', and it is only a warning by default. The
         maintenance contract in CLAUDE.md cannot survive warnings that scroll past. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The browser (wasm) compile gate, repeated here because this file BLOCKS the one that
    would otherwise supply it.

    MSBuild walks up from a project directory and stops at the first Directory.Build.props
    it finds. When this repo sits inside another as a submodule - it now does, at
    agg-sharp/Submodules/manifold-sharp, since agg-sharp's PolygonMesh took a
    ProjectReference on ManifoldSharp - this file is that first hit, so the outer repo's
    root Directory.Build.props never evaluates for anything under here. MatterCAD's says as
    much in its own header: it expects to cover "everything under the root - including the
    agg-sharp submodule, which has no Directory.Build.props of its own". This repo does, so
    the walk stops one level short of the boolean kernel.

    What that costs without this block: .github/workflows/browser-core-build.yml builds the
    library chain with -p:BrowserCompatGate=true so that CA1416 becomes an error and any
    API annotated [UnsupportedOSPlatform("browser")] that is reached unguarded fails in CI
    rather than when the wasm head is built. Global -p: properties DO flow into this project
    (they reach the whole graph), but SupportedPlatform is an ItemGroup, and items do not -
    so the property arrived and the item did not, and the analyzer had no browser platform
    to check against. The kernel would have been the one link in the chain nobody policed,
    which matters more here than elsewhere: this assembly exists to run in a browser, and
    IsAotCompatible in ManifoldSharp.csproj is the promise it keeps there.

    Deliberately a copy rather than an import of the outer file: this repo builds standalone
    from its own solution too, where there is no outer file to import, and a conditional
    import on a path that only exists in one checkout layout is worse than eight lines.
    Everything is conditioned on the property, so a normal build is unchanged.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(BrowserCompatGate)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA1416</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(BrowserCompatGate)' == 'true'">
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
